id: k8s.kubelet
kind: kubernetes
title: kubelet
aliases: kubelet, node agent
tags: architecture, control-plane
questions:
  - what does the kubelet do?
invariant: The kubelet is the per-node agent that runs the PodSpecs assigned to its node and reports their status back.
answer_30s: The kubelet watches assigned Pods, starts containers through the container runtime, runs probes, mounts volumes and reports status -- it is the only component that actually runs containers.
answer_2m: |
  Everything else in the control plane is bookkeeping; the kubelet is the
  executor. It owns the container runtime interface (CRI), the network plugin
  (CNI) invocation, volume mounting, probe execution, and cgroup enforcement of
  requests/limits. That placement explains the failure shapes: probe failures
  are kubelet decisions, OOMKill is the kernel reacting to a kubelet-configured
  limit, NodeNotReady is the kubelet stopping its heartbeat, and image pulls
  happen here (so imagePullBackOff is a node-level problem with registry
  credentials and network).
when_to_use:
  - understanding node-level failures, probes, evictions and image pulls
commands:
  - kubectl describe node <node> => kubelet version, conditions, eviction pressure
  - kubectl get events --field-selector reason=NodeNotReady => kubelet heartbeat losses
failure_modes:
  - node NotReady because the kubelet or runtime is unhealthy
  - image pull failures from node-level registry credentials
related:
  - k8s.scheduler
  - k8s.api-server
  - k8s.probe.liveness
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/
